{
  "_about": "Seed for scripts/gen-identities.py: what varies between real users of one browser version besides the phone model. Every list names its source; nothing here is a guess. Chrome builds: chromiumdash.appspot.com/fetch_releases (Stable, Windows), read 2026-09-22; 154 completed and 155/156 added 2026-10-10. Windows platform versions: Sec-CH-UA-Platform-Version is the Windows.Foundation.UniversalApiContract version (learn.microsoft.com/microsoft-edge/web-platform/how-to-detect-win11; Chromium components/embedder_support/user_agent_utils.cc, highest known contract 19) — 10.0.0 for Windows 10 22H2 measured on the capture machine (STAGE19), 13/14/15 for Windows 11 21H2/22H2/23H2, 19.0.0 for 24H2. macOS versions: Wikipedia release tables of macOS Sequoia (15) and Tahoe (26), read 2026-09-22; Chromium reports the version with dots. iOS versions: Wikipedia release tables of iOS 18 and iOS 26, read 2026-09-22; Safari 18 on iOS writes the OS version into the User-Agent twice, iPhone OS 18_1_1 and Version/18.1.1 (real strings on useragents.io and user-agents.net). Safari 26 froze the OS token at 18_7 and writes the real version only in Version/: every 26.0, 26.0.1, 26.1, 26.2, 26.3 and 26.5 observed in real traffic (user-agents.net, useragents.io, deviceandbrowserinfo.com, read 2026-09-22) says iPhone OS 18_7; frozen_os carries that. Updated 2026-09-26: Chrome 153 builds .37 .48 .54 .55 and the 154 builds (chromiumdash, Stable); macOS platform versions with three parts, as Chromium formats them (%d.%d.%d in user_agent_utils.cc GetPlatformVersion), and macOS 27.0 (Apple silicon only); Safari 26.0 on iOS pins the OS token at 18_6 (Leenheer, 51Degrees), 26.0.1 and later at 18_7. Updated 2026-10-29: the 155 and 156 builds completed and 157 added (chromiumdash, Stable, Windows). Added 2026-10-29: machines, the computer behind a desktop identity for the hints a site may ask for besides the User-Agent ones (STAGE45). The first Windows machine is the capture machine, measured: 1920x1080 at 100% (DPR 1), 16104 MB of RAM that Chromium reports as 16, the dark theme, and a maximized window's viewport 1920x969 — the 1040 px work area less the 71 px of Chrome's own UI, which is the restored window's 95 px (fpcapture, outerHeight - innerHeight) less the two 8 px frame borders and the 8 px the tab strip gives up when maximized. The others are a spread of common desktop screens and scale factors, not a measurement: RAM as Chromium rounds it (the nearest power of two, 2 to 32 GB), the viewport by the same rule over a 40 px Windows 10 taskbar or a 48 px Windows 11 one, a third of them dark. macOS and Linux machines carry no viewport: no window was measured there, and Chromium itself omits the viewport hints when it does not know the size. The network estimate (rtt 100 ms, downlink 1.6 Mbps, ect 4g) is the capture machine's; Chromium adds its per-site noise on top.",
